 doesn't have  the majority in parliament  this would  only have a limited direct impact on  what he can do in europe because he and  his team could still pitch  the ideas and try to  advance things on the level of the  council but also  through the parliament so that would not  be directly affected and also when it  comes to defense  from the first side that would also work  however we have to bear in mind that for  example the french parliament has to  authorize every deployment of the french  armed forces that goes beyond  four months and particularly with view  to a potential deployment of french  forces or a potential new deployment of  french forces to the sahel because you  know that france is now withdrawing from  mali but discussions are already ongoing  how european involvement and also french  involvement in region could look like  that could indeed become  a factor of  concern or of major interest in brussels  and also in other member states and  overall we have to bear in mind that  particularly if macron does not win  the presidential majority the  parliamentary majority  building consensus  with the other parties and having good  relations and working relations with  parliament to get domestic  agenda points implemented will take a  lot of presidential resources and that  are potentially resources that will then  be missing on the european level so  let's see how relations between macro  and parliament will evolve let's see  when whether the french as they used to  do  um  equip him with the parliamentary  majority to govern but i am  currently not very  optimistic that his particular  republican march will have this majority  thomas looked to me like you were  reacting to some of what jazeen was  saying there did you want to jump in  oh  respectfully yes  if we look at the french parliamentary  elections your audience needs to know  that for the past two decades now we  have had parliamentary elections a few  weeks after the presidential election  and here's what happened again and again  and again  um  all  uh electorates all voting blocks if i  may uh  got demobilized you know really really a  drop of the of the voting rate  but  the  voters that voted for the winner of the  presidential election they demobilized  way less than the others  so in terms of actual votes casted  what you see is a landslide in favor of  the guy who won the presidential  election so if things keep being the  same and they have been so for the past  20 years  we will see a landslide majority in  favor of emmanuel macron in the next  parliamentary elections  not because there is an overwhelming  support in his favor but just because of  what we call in french abstention  differential which would be i think in  english  the difference of abstention rate  between the different blocks after the  presidential election  aurelion um marine le pen in her speech  after the results were announced said  that she was going to fight on to secure  a large number of representatives in  these legislative elections does it look  to you like her party could do quite  well in those elections  no i i don't believe so i think i think  thomas summarized the kind of wider  context uh very well and i think it's  it's it's even more difficult for for  the rasam roman national and the far  right in general because they are again  facing a two-round election  uh and generally in the parliamentary  election not only there is more  abstention that tends to benefit of his  differential abstention but tends to  benefit the the party of the of the  newly elected president uh but there  also tends to be more alliances in the  second round against the far right and  and parties uh dropping uh off uh from  uh from the contention um to to make  sure that the far right doesn't go  through and this is why the foreign  national and before national have only  ever had a handful of of seats in  parliament except in 1986 when uh the uh  this curtain was changed to a  proportional vote um so so no i don't  think she will do very well but i think  it will still be interesting to see  actually if she manages to keep her  electorate mobilized uh or whether  actually uh a lot of about electorate  that she got uh this time around may  have been also protest and and and will  turn towards either abstention uh or  towards other parties um so i think i  think there will be lessons to be  learned but i cannot imagine that the  assembler national will do particularly  well in the parliamentary elections and  and aurelia and i i just want to ask you  about something you were kind of  touching on there i mean  the idea of this republican front that  people will join together to to try to  prevent a far-right candidate from  winning the presidency is that something  that's that's been stripped away by by  the result of this election i mean is  that a concept that's sort of crumbling  right now or or is it still strong  it is certainly something that is  crumbling and i think this is something  that has been crumbling for quite a long  time now um the  this republican this republican front is  no longer as strong as it used to be in  2002 for example and you know we've  noticed more and more of the  presidential candidates calling to vote  for le pen we had one in 2017 we had two  this time around  uh i think there's more and more  hesitation as well on the on the right  wing uh of a political spectrum uh with  regard to alliances so i think it will  be again  one of the lessons of this parliamentary  election to see uh what kind of  alliances come out  i suspect there will still be a  semblance of a republican front but but  will be strong enough to prevent before  national from gaining too many mps but  again i think it will be very important  to keep an eye on on actually how  mainstream assemblyman national is  becoming and how weak the republican  front is becoming as well as a result of  the mainstreaming of far-right politics  in france  partly partly because of the actions of  the mainstream itself rather than just  because of the actions of the far right  jasin from your standpoint how much has  the war in ukraine impacted  what went on in this election cycle and  the result of the election  um basically we saw when macro or won  the war in ukraine erupted in um or when  russia invaded ukraine uh in february uh  2022 then we saw that there was kind of  a rally around the flag effect so we saw  popularity scores on intentional vote  for macro  skyrocket but later on it dropped down  and that is basically a classical rally  around the flag effect that we see here  meaning that leaders in times of crisis  or in times of acute crisis  often  are given a bit more credit or have a  bit more popularity among the public  than they would have normally but we saw  that macron's score then dropped down a  little bit or to the almost to almost to  the level that it had before so i  wouldn't say that it has  significantly impacted the campaign  because we have seen that the major  topic in france here is  the coast of living however it had of  course an impact even if that impact was  most likely not as significant as the  debates on the course of living because  we saw for example during the um debate  between the pen and macro that le pen  was really struggling to defend herself  against um the points brought up by  mccall regarding the loan she still has  with the russian bank her  or her  relations with vladimir putin that he  has that she has been  maintaining since quite a few years so  there that definitely has played a role  and when it comes to the question of  capability of french leadership  i assume that that also has played a  role because mccall has already  manifested that he has the capacity to  lead in that kind of coughing so that  might have benefited him but in the end  as i said the dominating topic here was  the cost of living  from your perspective what are some of  the biggest challenges th